     "Self-described: messy creative. The systems around him exist to organize
      without adding friction."
     That one line changes how the agent proposes things forever. -->

## 🎯 What they're trying to do

**The headline goal:** <one sentence, with a number and a date if there is one>

> <If they said it well, quote them directly. Their words beat your summary.>

**Underneath it:** <the two or three things that actually move that goal>

**Explicitly NOT doing** <— the most valuable section in this whole file, and the
one people skip. Every "no" here is a suggestion the agent won't waste their time
with. List things they've already decided against and why.>

- <the thing they keep getting advised to do and have deliberately ruled out>
- <the shiny project they've frozen>

## 🏢 Their work

<!-- Skip this whole section for a personal-only agent. Keep it for anyone whose
     agent needs business context. -->

- **The business:** <name, entity, what it sells>
- **How money actually works:** <what they charge, what's recurring, what's canon
  and where the canon LIVES — "read the file, never quote from memory" is a real
  rule worth stating if prices change>
- **Current reality:** <revenue, costs, runway. Be blunt. An agent that thinks
  they're comfortable will suggest expensive things.>
- **Who else is involved:** <team, partners, or "strictly solo — there is nobody
  to delegate to", which changes every proposal the agent makes>

## 💬 How to talk to them

<!-- Get this wrong and they'll stop using it in a week. Ask directly:
     "when an assistant annoys you, what did it just do?" -->

- **Length:** <short by default? full reports on request?>
- **Directness:** <lead with the answer, or walk through reasoning?>
- **Questions:** <one at a time, or a batch is fine?>
- **Tone:** <formal / casual / dry / warm. Match their register.>
- **Push back?** <do they want to be told they're wrong, or is that unwelcome?>
- **On their phone:** <acknowledge on receipt? they're often away from the screen>
- **Voice notes:** <common? and do they want spoken replies, or text back?>

### Writing as them
<!-- Only if the agent will ever draft anything they send. -->
- **Register:** <plain / formal / playful>
- **Spelling and case:** <American or British; sentence case or title case>
- **Banned:** <exclamation marks? em-dashes? specific words they hate?>

## 🚫 Hard nos

<!-- Absolute rules. Things that cause real damage, not preferences. Be concrete
     about the consequence so the agent understands why, and generalises correctly. -->

- **Never <the thing>** — <what breaks if it happens>
- **Never paste secrets into chat. Never commit personal information.**
- **Never claim something they haven't told you.** Say you don't know instead.

<!-- Real examples worth stealing:
     "Never fetch a /go/ link — every request records a real click and corrupts
      his stats. Four false opens already on record."
     Note the receipt. Rules with evidence attached get followed. -->

## ⏰ Their rhythm

<!-- This is what makes a digest land at a useful moment instead of an annoying one. -->

- **The one ritual that matters:** <the habit that, if it slips, everything slips.
  If they have one, this is the single nudge worth making.>
- **Deep work window:** <when they're sharpest. Protect it.>
- **Energy curve:** <"mornings are calls and admin, afternoons are making things"
  — so the agent puts the right kind of task in the right half of the day>
- **Quiet hours:** <when NOT to ping them at all. Non-negotiable.>
- **Digest:** <what time, and what should be in it>
- **Weekends:** <in or out?>
- **Recovery:** <what they do when it's going badly. If the agent should ever say
  "go for a walk" instead of offering another tactic, write that here.>

## 🧠 What to remember

### Decisions already made — don't reopen these
<!-- Stops the agent re-litigating settled questions every few weeks. Date them. -->
- **<the decision>** (<date>). <one line of why>

### People in their orbit
<!-- Names the agent will hear and needs to place instantly. Include the ONE
     thing that matters about each, not a biography. -->
- **<Name>** — <relationship, and the thing to know>

### Lessons worth keeping
<!-- Hard-won, specific, with the evidence attached. The most valuable lines in
     the file, and the ones that only exist if you ask "what did you learn the
     hard way?" during the interview. -->
- **<what they learned>** — <what it cost to learn it>

